{"duration_api_ms":101916,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"aa6ae429-2946-47c0-b5c2-0b21338a671b","total_cost_usd":0.39297920000000003,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":61482,"cache_read_input_tokens":63326,"output_tokens":9650,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":3683},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":61482,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3008,"cache_read_input_tokens":59004,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6800,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6800},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":37788,"outputTokens":18,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.037878,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":9650,"cacheReadInputTokens":63326,"cacheCreationInputTokens":61482,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.3551012,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":3683,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"lan-b-interface-zone\",\"requirement\":\"Bring node1's unconfigured lan-b-facing interface (vtnet2) into service as its own OPNsense zone with an address on lan-b\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"vtnet2 was administratively enabled, added as a new opt1/'LANB' interface in config.xml with static 10.60.60.1/24, and configctl interface reconfigure opt1 applied the config live, confirmed by ifconfig showing inet 10.60.60.1/24 and description 'LANB (opt1)' on vtnet2. A matching firewall pass rule for the new zone was also confirmed present via pfctl -s rules.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-54b99040644f4530\",\"cmd-79166914c2494342\",\"cmd-538b17ba3b294be5\"]},{\"id\":\"node3-reaches-node1\",\"requirement\":\"node3 reaches node1 on lan-b\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node3's ens7 was assigned 10.60.60.2/24 and directly pinged node1's 10.60.60.1 with 0% loss and TTL 64 (single hop), confirming reachability on the new lan-b segment.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-79ef66a78c1f4869\",\"cmd-4066404cc6994965\"]},{\"id\":\"node3-reaches-node2-through-node1\",\"requirement\":\"node3 reaches node2 on lan-a through node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"With a host route on node3 to 10.100.25.11 via node1's lan-b address and OPNsense's pre-existing automatic outbound NAT covering the new opt1/lan-b network, node3 successfully pinged node2 (10.100.25.11) with TTL 63 (one router hop), and node1's pf state table showed the ICMP flow being NAT-translated and forwarded, directly demonstrating traversal through node1.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-4066404cc6994965\",\"cmd-d19309961f304388\",\"cmd-8645bbb523464a3b\",\"cmd-96a5d084c0004de7\"]},{\"id\":\"node1-restart-recovery\",\"requirement\":\"(supplemental) node1 restart evidence: baseline, acknowledged restart, changed boot identity, recovered state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart boottime (17:31:33) captured, shutdown -r now acknowledged, two subsequent SSH attempts timed out confirming a real outage, then boottime changed to 17:41:09, and post-restart checks show the opt1 interface, its firewall rule, and node3/node2 reachability through node1 all recovered.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-30ff26684ddf4bc4\",\"cmd-f88a5ee80f4b43d0\",\"cmd-c1e1a819bba340cb\",\"cmd-c2b4ce6c7a14401f\",\"cmd-63f6c2a310c44a55\",\"cmd-2a8d26767adb4370\"]},{\"id\":\"node2-restart-recovery\",\"requirement\":\"(supplemental) node2 restart evidence: baseline, acknowledged restart, changed boot identity, recovered state\",\"status\":\"partially_satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart boot_id (2b3a8d87) captured, sudo shutdown -r now acknowledged, post-restart boot_id changed to 59760cb9 with new uptime, ens2 address and node3->node2-through-node1 connectivity recovered. However, unlike node1, no intermediate timed-out/failed connection attempt was captured to directly confirm the outage window, so the bounded-disruption portion of the transition is inferred from timestamps rather than directly observed.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-67a36613ebe14716\",\"cmd-2b1598eac655424d\",\"cmd-e59e0cbc68d8477c\",\"cmd-3e8ad6b6cf2d4dcd\",\"cmd-64f7394e89004c08\"]},{\"id\":\"node3-restart-recovery\",\"requirement\":\"(supplemental) node3 restart evidence: baseline, acknowledged restart, changed boot identity, recovered state\",\"status\":\"partially_satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart boot_id (88feca03) captured, sudo shutdown -r now acknowledged, post-restart boot_id changed to 1e873999, and recovered state shows persisted ens7 static IP/route from /etc/network/interfaces.d and successful pings to both node1 and node2. As with node2, no direct outage/connection-timeout evidence was captured during the restart window.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-87afad8a42b14c96\",\"cmd-5bca5127ab9f41ea\",\"cmd-a1d3186a0d6e4760\",\"cmd-1d50a3f057d34a9c\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"The executor performed several bounded, diagnostic mutations to identify which unconfigured NIC (vtnet1 vs vtnet2) on node1 faced lan-b vs lan-a: temporary IPs 172.30.30.11/24 and 172.30.30.12/24 were added to node2's and node3's ens7 interfaces (cmd-1dc90aa1e02d4aa2, cmd-2f30094df1c14ccc, cmd-ad6e188423c54236, cmd-7454e2a25ef249b2) to generate broadcast/ARP traffic captured via tcpdump on node1's vtnet1/vtnet2, correctly mapping vtnet2 to node3/lan-b. These temporary addresses were fully removed afterward (cmd-3ee7f84821da4114, cmd-469b1979d67a4f1f), and node2's unused ens7 was returned to a down state (cmd-bedf2673988c49ef) since it played no role in node2's final configuration. No residue, failed services, or unsafe exposure remained in the after-executor lifecycle snapshot; temp-file listings show only expected session/reboot artifacts. The interface-mapping approach was a reasonable, low-risk way to resolve real ambiguity between two unconfigured NICs before making the final config.xml edit, and all test state was cleanly reverted.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-1dc90aa1e02d4aa2\",\"cmd-2f30094df1c14ccc\",\"cmd-ad6e188423c54236\",\"cmd-7454e2a25ef249b2\",\"cmd-3ee7f84821da4114\",\"cmd-469b1979d67a4f1f\",\"cmd-bedf2673988c49ef\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All three material outcomes of the public task were satisfied with direct, corroborating evidence: node1's unconfigured lan-b-facing NIC (vtnet2) was brought up as a new OPNsense opt1/'LANB' zone with static 10.60.60.1/24 and a firewall pass rule; node3 was configured with 10.60.60.2/24 and directly pinged node1 successfully (TTL64); and node3 reached node2 on lan-a through node1 via a host route plus OPNsense's automatic outbound NAT, verified end-to-end with a successful ping (TTL63) and corroborating pf NAT state-table entries. The independent provider-wide restart protocol was executed on all three nodes with pre/post boot-identity changes and post-restart functional recovery confirmed for all; node1's restart additionally included direct evidence of a connection-timeout outage window, while node2 and node3 lacked that same direct outage confirmation. Diagnostic mutations used to disambiguate node1's two unconfigured NICs were bounded and fully reverted, leaving no residue.\",\"limitations\":[\"The exact contents of the base64-encoded Python script used to edit /conf/config.xml (cmd-9c22d783e1af4731) were not directly inspected in the trace; its effect was instead verified indirectly through subsequent config reads and live interface/firewall state, which is sufficient but not a full byte-level confirmation of the edit.\",\"node2 and node3 restarts lack a captured intermediate failed/timed-out connection attempt (unlike node1), so the bounded-disruption portion of their restart transitions is inferred from boot-identity and uptime changes rather than directly observed mid-outage.\",\"Reachability from node3 to node2 depends on NAT and a manually-added host route on node3 rather than symmetric routing on node2; this works as demonstrated but was not tested for reverse-initiated traffic from node2 to node3, which the task did not require.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"lan-b-interface-zone","requirement":"Bring node1's unconfigured lan-b-facing interface (vtnet2) into service as its own OPNsense zone with an address on lan-b","status":"satisfied","summary":"vtnet2 was administratively enabled, added as a new opt1/'LANB' interface in config.xml with static 10.60.60.1/24, and configctl interface reconfigure opt1 applied the config live, confirmed by ifconfig showing inet 10.60.60.1/24 and description 'LANB (opt1)' on vtnet2. A matching firewall pass rule for the new zone was also confirmed present via pfctl -s rules.","evidence_ids":["cmd-54b99040644f4530","cmd-79166914c2494342","cmd-538b17ba3b294be5"]},{"id":"node3-reaches-node1","requirement":"node3 reaches node1 on lan-b","status":"satisfied","summary":"node3's ens7 was assigned 10.60.60.2/24 and directly pinged node1's 10.60.60.1 with 0% loss and TTL 64 (single hop), confirming reachability on the new lan-b segment.","evidence_ids":["cmd-79ef66a78c1f4869","cmd-4066404cc6994965"]},{"id":"node3-reaches-node2-through-node1","requirement":"node3 reaches node2 on lan-a through node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"With a host route on node3 to 10.100.25.11 via node1's lan-b address and OPNsense's pre-existing automatic outbound NAT covering the new opt1/lan-b network, node3 successfully pinged node2 (10.100.25.11) with TTL 63 (one router hop), and node1's pf state table showed the ICMP flow being NAT-translated and forwarded, directly demonstrating traversal through node1.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4066404cc6994965","cmd-d19309961f304388","cmd-8645bbb523464a3b","cmd-96a5d084c0004de7"]},{"id":"node1-restart-recovery","requirement":"(supplemental) node1 restart evidence: baseline, acknowledged restart, changed boot identity, recovered state","status":"satisfied","summary":"Pre-restart boottime (17:31:33) captured, shutdown -r now acknowledged, two subsequent SSH attempts timed out confirming a real outage, then boottime changed to 17:41:09, and post-restart checks show the opt1 interface, its firewall rule, and node3/node2 reachability through node1 all recovered.","evidence_ids":["cmd-30ff26684ddf4bc4","cmd-f88a5ee80f4b43d0","cmd-c1e1a819bba340cb","cmd-c2b4ce6c7a14401f","cmd-63f6c2a310c44a55","cmd-2a8d26767adb4370"]},{"id":"node2-restart-recovery","requirement":"(supplemental) node2 restart evidence: baseline, acknowledged restart, changed boot identity, recovered state","status":"partially_satisfied","summary":"Pre-restart boot_id (2b3a8d87) captured, sudo shutdown -r now acknowledged, post-restart boot_id changed to 59760cb9 with new uptime, ens2 address and node3->node2-through-node1 connectivity recovered. However, unlike node1, no intermediate timed-out/failed connection attempt was captured to directly confirm the outage window, so the bounded-disruption portion of the transition is inferred from timestamps rather than directly observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-67a36613ebe14716","cmd-2b1598eac655424d","cmd-e59e0cbc68d8477c","cmd-3e8ad6b6cf2d4dcd","cmd-64f7394e89004c08"]},{"id":"node3-restart-recovery","requirement":"(supplemental) node3 restart evidence: baseline, acknowledged restart, changed boot identity, recovered state","status":"partially_satisfied","summary":"Pre-restart boot_id (88feca03) captured, sudo shutdown -r now acknowledged, post-restart boot_id changed to 1e873999, and recovered state shows persisted ens7 static IP/route from /etc/network/interfaces.d and successful pings to both node1 and node2. As with node2, no direct outage/connection-timeout evidence was captured during the restart window.","evidence_ids":["cmd-87afad8a42b14c96","cmd-5bca5127ab9f41ea","cmd-a1d3186a0d6e4760","cmd-1d50a3f057d34a9c"]}],"confidence":0.9,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.85,"summary":"The executor performed several bounded, diagnostic mutations to identify which unconfigured NIC (vtnet1 vs vtnet2) on node1 faced lan-b vs lan-a: temporary IPs 172.30.30.11/24 and 172.30.30.12/24 were added to node2's and node3's ens7 interfaces (cmd-1dc90aa1e02d4aa2, cmd-2f30094df1c14ccc, cmd-ad6e188423c54236, cmd-7454e2a25ef249b2) to generate broadcast/ARP traffic captured via tcpdump on node1's vtnet1/vtnet2, correctly mapping vtnet2 to node3/lan-b. These temporary addresses were fully removed afterward (cmd-3ee7f84821da4114, cmd-469b1979d67a4f1f), and node2's unused ens7 was returned to a down state (cmd-bedf2673988c49ef) since it played no role in node2's final configuration. No residue, failed services, or unsafe exposure remained in the after-executor lifecycle snapshot; temp-file listings show only expected session/reboot artifacts. The interface-mapping approach was a reasonable, low-risk way to resolve real ambiguity between two unconfigured NICs before making the final config.xml edit, and all test state was cleanly reverted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1dc90aa1e02d4aa2","cmd-2f30094df1c14ccc","cmd-ad6e188423c54236","cmd-7454e2a25ef249b2","cmd-3ee7f84821da4114","cmd-469b1979d67a4f1f","cmd-bedf2673988c49ef","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All three material outcomes of the public task were satisfied with direct, corroborating evidence: node1's unconfigured lan-b-facing NIC (vtnet2) was brought up as a new OPNsense opt1/'LANB' zone with static 10.60.60.1/24 and a firewall pass rule; node3 was configured with 10.60.60.2/24 and directly pinged node1 successfully (TTL64); and node3 reached node2 on lan-a through node1 via a host route plus OPNsense's automatic outbound NAT, verified end-to-end with a successful ping (TTL63) and corroborating pf NAT state-table entries. The independent provider-wide restart protocol was executed on all three nodes with pre/post boot-identity changes and post-restart functional recovery confirmed for all; node1's restart additionally included direct evidence of a connection-timeout outage window, while node2 and node3 lacked that same direct outage confirmation. Diagnostic mutations used to disambiguate node1's two unconfigured NICs were bounded and fully reverted, leaving no residue.","limitations":["The exact contents of the base64-encoded Python script used to edit /conf/config.xml (cmd-9c22d783e1af4731) were not directly inspected in the trace; its effect was instead verified indirectly through subsequent config reads and live interface/firewall state, which is sufficient but not a full byte-level confirmation of the edit.","node2 and node3 restarts lack a captured intermediate failed/timed-out connection attempt (unlike node1), so the bounded-disruption portion of their restart transitions is inferred from boot-identity and uptime changes rather than directly observed mid-outage.","Reachability from node3 to node2 depends on NAT and a manually-added host route on node3 rather than symmetric routing on node2; this works as demonstrated but was not tested for reverse-initiated traffic from node2 to node3, which the task did not require."]},"ttft_ms":51063,"type":"result","duration_ms":100393,"uuid":"66ef8fe2-16df-44e1-9f00-4fdbd82ab9bd","ttft_stream_ms":2777,"time_to_request_ms":41,"first_content_frame_ms":3002,"queued_turn_count":0}
